RISE ASCII SCENE 7
#############################################################################################################
#                                      RISE Ascii Scene File                                                #
#                                                                                                           #
#   File:           weathered_workbench.RISEscene                                                          #
#   Description:    Textures showcase 1/4 (doc 88 Phase 1+2 arc) -- a wooden workbench with a bolted-on   #
#                   cast-iron bench vise and a de-tiled diamond-plate tool tray.  The TOP is eight          #
#                   boards of softwood, an sdf_geometry with real joints, driven by ONE                     #
#                   expression_painter field whose R channel colours and roughens it and whose G            #
#                   channel drives relief_modifier relief_wood.  The four legs carry the same wood at       #
#                   their own footprint, grain running up the post, plain.  The tool tray is a              #
#                   checker_painter hex-tiled via stochastic_tile_painter to break its grid repeat --       #
#                   no tileable photo/scan asset exists in-repo, so this demo substitutes a procedural      #
#                   checker source to exercise the de-tiling MECHANISM; the intended use of                 #
#                   stochastic_tile_painter is a small tiling photo/scan, per the skill.                    #
#                                                                                                           #
#   THE BENCH TOP.  Until 2026-09-10 this was a single box_geometry under one isotropic                     #
#   domain-warped fbm, with a scatter_painter of dark stamps multiplied on as "oil stains" --               #
#   and the standing criticism of it was exactly right: streaky yellow noise with black                     #
#   blotches, obviously a generated texture rather than something organic.  The vise beside it              #
#   had already been rebuilt to plank_closeup.RISEscene's seven-rule doctrine; the top is now               #
#   built to the same one, and the fbm, the scatter_painter and the blend_painter are gone, not             #
#   re-skinned.  (scatter_painter keeps its dedicated coverage in                                           #
#   scenes/Tests/Painters/stochastic_scatter.RISEscene.)                                                    #
#                                                                                                           #
#   1. THE FOOTPRINT WAS MEASURED FIRST, AND EVERY SIZE FOLLOWS FROM IT.  Painting `fw*15` on               #
#      the top as a lambertian under a flat white environment and dividing by a flat-albedo                 #
#      control gives, over the visible top face, fw p50 = 10.7 mm -- 8.1 mm at the near edge and            #
#      15.5 mm at the far one.  The gradient IS the design brief: this is a horizontal plane seen           #
#      from 14 degrees above it, so the perpendicular pixel spacing of 5.3-5.6 mm is divided by an          #
#      incidence cosine near 0.2.  The vise, measured the same way, reads 7.95 mm -- and the                #
#      FRONT EDGE of this same top reads about 1 mm, because it is seen near face-on.  One object           #
#      can be in two footprint regimes at once, and the cathedral figure that reads beautifully on          #
#      the front edge is exactly the figure that must be faded out on the top.                              #
#                                                                                                           #
#   2. WHAT A 10.7 mm PIXEL CAN SEE OF A BENCH TOP IS ITS BOARDS.  Growth rings are 4-7 mm apart:           #
#      half a pixel, and in colour they are the streaky noise the criticism named.  So the rings            #
#      go to ROUGHNESS alone (`sp_top_ring`), and the structure goes to eight boards -- 133 mm              #
#      wide, 12 px, separated by 5 mm joints that are REAL GEOMETRY -- each with its own pith               #
#      depth, pith offset, axis tilt, ring spacing and tone.  That per-board independence is the            #
#      single loudest cue that this is lumber: the figure on one board has nothing to do with its           #
#      neighbour's, and it STOPS at the joint.                                                              #
#                                                                                                           #
#   3. EXCEPT AT THE CATHEDRAL CROWNS, AND THAT EXCEPTION IS MEASURED, NOT TASTED.  Ring spacing            #
#      ON THE FACE is `ring_m / sin(theta)`, and at the crown of an arch theta goes to zero, so a           #
#      4.5 mm ring opens to 40-100 mm of face spacing.  `rfade` computes that face spacing                  #
#      analytically -- the ring coordinate's gradient projected into the surface is                         #
#      `sqrt(1 - (u.N)^2)` -- and fades the ring term in against `fw` itself.  Below ~1.15 px the           #
#      rings are roughness only; past ~2.6 they are colour, relief and roughness together.  The             #
#      same gate runs on the legs and admits MORE there, unaltered, because their `fw` is smaller.          #
#                                                                                                           #
#   4. ONE FIELD, BUT TWO CHANNELS.  `expr_top` returns a vec3: R is what the surface LOOKS like            #
#      (wood plus every process term), G is what it is SHAPED like (the continuous coarse                   #
#      undulation).  `ramp_top` and `sp_top_rough` read R; `sp_wood_relief` reads G.  This is one           #
#      evaluation and one field -- but the two consumers stop at different points in the same sum,          #
#      for a reason that cost four rounds and is recorded below.                                            #
#                                                                                                           #
#   5. PROCESS TIED TO GEOMETRY, AS ALWAYS.  `convexity(0.010)` polishes the board arrises and              #
#      the front edge a forearm rubs; the analytic distance to the board edge puts the crack and            #
#      its dirt in the joint; `proximity(0.02)` -- the cross-object signal -- keeps the grime the           #
#      vise's flange deposits; a hand-placed halo puts wear where the work is.  The rare events             #
#      are rare and hand-placed: two knots with the rings sweeping around them, one ring left by a          #
#      wet tin, one patch where oil soaked in.  Nothing is scattered.                                       #
#                                                                                                           #
#   THE VISE.  Until 2026-09-06 the "iron vise" here was two box_geometry under an isotropic worley3d       #
#   field reprojected by mapping_painter{projection world} -- and the standing criticism of it was exactly  #
#   right: it said nothing about what it was, and it looked like a generated texture rather than an         #
#   object.  It is now built against the seven-rule doctrine in the header of                               #
#   plank_closeup.RISEscene, whose rule 7 (silhouette first) named this vise as its counter-example.        #
#   The worley chunks and the mapping_painter are gone, not re-skinned.                                     #
#                                                                                                           #
#   1. THE FOOTPRINT WAS MEASURED FIRST, AND EVERY SIZE FOLLOWS FROM IT.  Painting clamp(fw*50,0,1) on      #
#      the vise as a lambertian and dividing by a flat-albedo control gives fw p50 = 7.95 mm over the       #
#      object (the geometric pixel spacing at its 3.38 m axial depth is 5.4 mm, so fw runs about 1.5 px     #
#      here).  That single number decides the whole surface: a feature needs ~3 px to carry shape, so the   #
#      casting undulation is 33 mm (6 px) and the paint chips 20-40 mm, and a real casting's 1-3 mm         #
#      pitting -- a fifth of a pixel -- is BANNED from colour and relief and sent to roughness alone.       #
#      This is why the vise carries no pits at all where the nail in plank_closeup carries plenty: the      #
#      two scenes are 20x apart in footprint, and the sizes are not transferable.                           #
#                                                                                                           #
#   2. THE SILHOUETTE SAYS "BENCH VISE" BEFORE ANY TEXTURE.  Fifteen sdf_geometry parts: a bolted base      #
#      flange with four proud bolt heads, a cast body carrying the fixed rear jaw with a flat anvil pad     #
#      behind it, a machined slide bar, a movable front jaw on it, two hardened jaw pads standing proud     #
#      of both castings, a lead screw, a thrust collar and a T-handle hanging off-centre in its hole.       #
#      The castings are smin-joined (k 0.014-0.030) so they carry the fillets a drawn casting has; the      #
#      bar, the pads and the bolt heads are HARD unions, because a machined part meets a casting at an      #
#      edge.  The object is yawed 48 degrees -- a swivel base is what a vise has one FOR -- which is the    #
#      only orientation that shows the jaw gap, the jaw faces and the full length of the handle at once.    #
#                                                                                                           #
#   3. ONE FIELD, FOUR CONSUMERS.  expr_vise runs a WEATHERING axis (machined steel -> paint -> the cast    #
#      iron a chip exposes -> rust); ramp_vise colours it, sp_vise_rough bridges it to GGX roughness,       #
#      sp_vise_relief bridges it raw into relief_vise, and sp_vise_ior / sp_vise_extinction bridge it into  #
#      the FRESNEL, so the same field that says "this is paint" also stops that point being a conductor.    #
#      Variation is tied to the geometry throughout: occlusion() puts grime and rust in the fillets and     #
#      under the flange lip, -N.y rusts the undersides, curv chips the paint off the drawn arrises, and a   #
#      Po-space part mask -- not a noise -- decides which faces are machined.                               #
#                                                                                                           #
#   ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------      #
#   WHAT WAS TRIED AND WAS WRONG                                                                            #
#   ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------      #
#                                                                                                           #
#   -- (BENCH TOP) THE RING-EDGE WARP IS DEAD AT THIS DISTANCE, AND WAS DELETED RATHER THAN LEFT IN.        #
#      It was authored exactly as the plank's -- 17 mm across the grain by 170 mm along, amplitude          #
#      held under a quarter ring -- and `fbm` fades the octaves the pixel footprint cannot resolve,         #
#      so at a 10.7 mm footprint it returned essentially zero everywhere.  Probing the field is what        #
#      caught it.  The same probe caught the COARSE FIGURE half-faded for the same reason: at               #
#      38 x 145 mm it was arriving at +-0.017 of field against an intended +-0.08, and the board            #
#      faces read blank.  At 77 x 285 mm it survives.  OCTAVE SIZE IS NOT A TASTE DECISION, and the         #
#      auto domain-scale fade means a term can be authored, correct, and entirely absent.                   #
#                                                                                                           #
#   -- (BENCH TOP) A PROCESS TERM WITH A HEIGHT IS WHAT MADE THE JOINTS BLACK.  Binding the whole           #
#      field raw to `relief_modifier` -- doctrine 5 as the plank states it -- put 591 near-black            #
#      pixels per 10000 on this top, every one of them on a joint line or the near arris, and it was        #
#      misread for three rounds as the joints being geometrically too deep.  They were not: the joint       #
#      dirt is a 0.28 step across 10 mm, the clamp binds at full tilt over it, and at 78 degrees of         #
#      incidence a full-tilt away-facing normal is past the geometric horizon.  Splitting the field         #
#      into R (look) and G (shape) channels took it to ZERO.  The general rule the plank's doctrine 5       #
#      needs, and this scene is where it showed up: one field, yes -- but a term that is a FACT ABOUT       #
#      THE SURFACE'S HISTORY is not a term about its SHAPE, and on a grazing surface the difference         #
#      is the whole near-black budget.                                                                      #
#                                                                                                           #
#   -- (BENCH TOP) AND THE CORRESPONDING TRAP ONE LEVEL DOWN: a PER-BOARD term in the relief channel        #
#      is also a height step, because the relief stencil (`fw`/2 = 5.4 mm) is wider than the 5 mm           #
#      joint and straddles the board index within a centimetre of every joint.  Offsetting the              #
#      figure's noise domain per board -- which is exactly what makes the figure stop at the joint,         #
#      and is right in COLOUR -- cost 270 near-black per 10000 when it reached relief.  `figc` is           #
#      the same undulation without the break, and it is what channel G carries.                             #
#                                                                                                           #
#   -- (BENCH TOP) CHASING JOINTS THAT WERE NEVER THERE.  Three rounds were spent tuning the joint          #
#      GEOMETRY (8 mm gap 10 mm deep: near-black slots, a duckboard; 3 mm gap 2 mm deep: invisible)         #
#      before the relief bisect showed that the joints which had "read" at 8 mm were the relief             #
#      artefact above, and the real 3 mm groove had been antialiased out of existence the whole time.       #
#      A 3 mm feature at a 10.7 mm footprint cannot be seen, and no amount of tuning a thing that is        #
#      a third of a pixel wide will make it appear.  MEASURE THE FEATURE AGAINST `fw` BEFORE TUNING         #
#      IT.  What ships is 5 mm wide and 5 mm deep between 9 mm eased arrises -- 2.1 px -- and most of       #
#      the dark line you see is the DIRT, with the geometry there to put that dirt in a real crease         #
#      and to give `convexity()` an arris to find.                                                          #
#                                                                                                           #
#   -- (BENCH TOP) THE JOINT DIRT WAS KEYED TO AN `edge` VALUE THE FLAT SURFACE NEVER REACHES.              #
#      `edge` runs 0 at the board's centre line to 1 at the joint, but the board's flat top stops at        #
#      0.834 and the rest is the eased arris -- where the N.y gate that keeps dirt off the vertical         #
#      faces correctly turns it OFF.  A crack band at `edge` 0.90-1.00 therefore painted nothing at         #
#      all.  A band keyed on a normalised coordinate has to be checked against the range the SURFACE        #
#      actually occupies, not the range the coordinate nominally spans.                                     #
#                                                                                                           #
#   -- (BENCH TOP) THE RAMP REFUSED TO SPEND THE RANGE THE FIELD OCCUPIED.  Holding the early-wood          #
#      plateau flat from 0.52 to 0.88 is right for the RING gradient (the plank's "row of rods"), but       #
#      the field's bulk variation here is board tone and coarse figure, which ARE meant to be tonal.        #
#      The bulk of the top sat in 1.35:1 of luminance and read airbrushed.  Respacing the stops so          #
#      0.48-0.80 covers 1.9:1 -- with plateau-to-late-wood at 2.9:1 and the darkest stops still             #
#      reserved -- is what made the boards read as separate pieces of wood.                                 #
#                                                                                                           #
#   -- (BENCH TOP) THE OLD RAMP'S PALE STOP WAS 0.78 0.55 0.30, AN R:B OF 2.6, AND RENDERED AS              #
#      CARTOON ORANGE.  Aged, dust-greyed softwood is nearer 1.7, which is what ships.  Saturation is       #
#      a "generated" tell in its own right, independently of contrast.                                      #
#                                                                                                           #
#   -- THE FIRST VISE WAS BUILT AT TRUE SCALE AND WAS ILLEGIBLE.  A 200 mm-tall vise is 37 px here and      #
#      read as a grey lump.  The shipped one is 300 mm above the bench -- deliberately a big shop vise,     #
#      and deliberately the same envelope the two boxes it replaced occupied, so the composition, the       #
#      bench top's relief tuning and the camera did not have to move.                                       #
#                                                                                                           #
#   -- THE HANDLE POINTED AT THE CAMERA.  With the screw axis along +Z the T-handle is 28 degrees off       #
#      the view axis and foreshortens to 47% -- a stub.  Solving for a bar PERPENDICULAR to the view        #
#      (0.898*sin(t) + 0.19*cos(t) = 0 at the 48-degree yaw) gives a near-vertical hang, which then runs    #
#      the bar's lower end into the bench top; -35 degrees is the compromise that keeps 93% of the length   #
#      on screen with the end still 49 mm clear.  Foreshortening is a composition variable, not a detail.   #
#                                                                                                           #
#   -- EVERY fbm HAD OCTAVES BELOW THE PIXEL FOOTPRINT, AND THEY READ AS DIRT.  fbm(P*30, 3, ...) puts      #
#      its third octave at 8 mm, and fbm(P*32, 4, ...) its last two at 7 and 3 mm -- all under two          #
#      pixels.  The field probe looked correctly mottled at a distance and turned to fine speckle up        #
#      close, which is precisely the "generated" tell.  OCTAVE COUNT IS A FEATURE-SIZE DECISION, not a      #
#      quality dial: both fields are 2 octaves now, and the sub-pixel structure that was cut from them      #
#      lives in sp_vise_grain, which reaches roughness ONLY.                                                #
#                                                                                                           #
#   -- THE RAMP GAVE PAINT AND BARE IRON THE SAME LUMINANCE.  Being careful about the doctrine's            #
#      over-contrast warning, the first ramp put intact paint at 0.090 and the iron a chip exposes at       #
#      0.070 -- a 1.3:1 step that is invisible under any lighting.  The field was healthy the whole time    #
#      (a probe showed a 0.062 within-band standard deviation); the RAMP was eating it.  Over-contrast is   #
#      a tell, but so is a ramp that refuses to spend the range the field actually occupies -- the shipped  #
#      step is 2.3:1, which is the same band the plank's early-to-late wood uses.                           #
#                                                                                                           #
#   -- THE PART MASK FADED OUT ACROSS THE OUTER HALF OF EACH JAW PAD.  padband's z transition was set at    #
#      the pad's own boundary rather than just outside it, so the pad's outer half sat at mask ~0.5, took   #
#      half the CAST branch, and grew a rust fringe along the top of every pad.  A part mask has to         #
#      OVERSHOOT the part; the transition then lands on the neighbouring casting, where it is hidden by     #
#      the crease that is already there.  Every mask edge in expr_vise is placed on a real geometric edge   #
#      for that reason, and it matters twice over because relief_vise reads the same field, so a mask       #
#      transition is also a height step.                                                                    #
#                                                                                                           #
#   -- CHIPS KEYED MOSTLY ON `curv` DREW AN OUTLINE.  At 0.55*arris the paint failed in a neat band         #
#      around every jaw and flange edge, which reads as a rendered outline effect rather than as wear.      #
#      The arris still has to be the FIRST place paint goes, but at 0.18 against a 1.4x chip field and a    #
#      0.35 gravity-run term, so the chips are patches that happen to reach the edges.                      #
#                                                                                                           #
#   -- RUST WITH NO DIRECTION.  Rust is the one process here that gravity writes, so `runs` is an 8:1       #
#      vertical fbm (11 mm across, 88 mm down) that both elongates the chips downward and concentrates      #
#      the rust in the lower part of each. The casting undulation is left ISOTROPIC on purpose -- a sand    #
#      mould has no grain direction -- which is the one place this scene departs from the doctrine's        #
#      "every noise term gets a direction", and it departs because the physics says so.                     #
#                                                                                                           #
#   -- A RUST STAIN RUNNING OVER INTACT PAINT IS NOT REACHABLE FROM A SINGLE 1-D RAMP, and was dropped      #
#      rather than faked: a point cannot be both "paint" and "rust" on one axis.  Rust here only grows      #
#      where the paint is already gone, which is what the physics says anyway.                              #
#                                                                                                           #
#   -- GRIME UNDER THE FLANGE ON THE BENCH WAS "OUT OF REACH -- occlusion() IS SELF-OCCLUSION               #
#      ONLY", AND THAT IS NO LONGER TRUE.  It was the right refusal at the time: every signal               #
#      this scene had asked the hit geometry about ITSELF, so the bench top could not see the               #
#      vise standing on it, and the only alternative was a painted decal.  Since 2026-09-08                 #
#      `proximity(r)` measures the distance to the nearest surface of any OTHER object, and                 #
#      the term is real -- see `expr_top`, which carries it, and note that its radius is a                  #
#      WORLD LENGTH (20 mm) where occlusion's is a fraction of the object.  The measured                    #
#      profile outward from the flange's touchdown line is in that chunk's own comment.                     #
#      What is still NOT painted is grime on the FLOOR at the legs' feet: the floor is a                    #
#      deliberately flat `uniformcolor_painter` (a supporting surface, out of the depth of                  #
#      interest), and making it an expression painter to carry one dust band is a bigger                    #
#      change than the band is worth.  The LEG side of that same seam does get the term for                 #
#      free, since the legs carry `mat_wood_leg`, which runs the same `proximity(0.02)`.                    #
#                                                                                                           #
#   Author:         RISE contributors                                                                       #
#   Birthdate:      2026-08-20                                                                              #
#                                                                                                           #
#############################################################################################################

##################################################
# Setup a renderer
##################################################

# Declared before the rasterizer chunk below (which references it as
# `radiance_map`) -- ordinary declare-before-use.  Refinement pass: the
# first render left an unlit white void above the bench; a soft workshop-
# window sky reads far better and costs nothing extra to render.
uniformcolor_painter
{
	name			pnt_sky
	color			0.58 0.60 0.64
}

standard_shader
{
	name			global
	shaderop		DefaultDirectLighting
}

pixelpel_rasterizer
{
	max_recursion			4
	samples					12
	lum_samples				1
	radiance_map			pnt_sky
	radiance_background		TRUE
}

file_rasterizeroutput
{
	pattern			rendered/weathered_workbench
	type			PNG
	bpp				8
	color_space		sRGB
}

##################################################
# Cameras
##################################################

film
{
	width			640
	height			480
}

pinhole_camera
{
	location		0 1.6 3.4
	lookat			0 0.75 0
	up				0 1 0
	fov				42.0
}

##################################################
# Painters -- THE BENCH TOP.  One wood field per board, three consumers.
#
# The FOOTPRINT WAS MEASURED FIRST (doctrine 1), by painting `fw*15` on the
# top as a lambertian under a flat white environment, dividing by a
# flat-albedo control and reading the pixels back:
#
#     fw over the visible top face   p50 = 10.7 mm   (p10 7.8, p90 15.9)
#     ... at the near edge                  8.1 mm
#     ... at the far edge                  15.5 mm
#     ... on the front edge face            ~1 mm   (seen near face-on)
#
# The gradient is the whole story: the top is a horizontal plane seen from
# 14 degrees above it, so the perpendicular pixel spacing (5.3-5.6 mm) is
# divided by an incidence cosine of about 0.2 and the footprint runs 8 mm
# near to 15 mm far.  A feature needs ~3 px to carry shape, so on this
# surface that is 32 mm at the middle of the top and 46 mm at the back.
#
# WHAT FOLLOWS FROM THAT, and it is most of this chunk's design:
#
#   GROWTH RINGS ARE SUB-PIXEL AND CANNOT BE COLOUR.  Softwood rings are
#   4-7 mm apart radially; at 10.7 mm per pixel they are half a pixel and
#   putting them in the albedo produces exactly the streaky noise this
#   scene used to have.  They go into ROUGHNESS (`sp_top_ring`), where a
#   sub-pixel band modulates gloss instead of aliasing into dirt.
#
#   EXCEPT AT THE CATHEDRAL CROWNS, WHICH ARE NOT SUB-PIXEL.  Ring spacing
#   ON THE FACE is `ring_m / sin(theta)` for theta the angle between the
#   ring cylinder and the surface, and at the crown of a cathedral arch
#   theta goes to zero -- the same 4.5 mm ring opens to 40-100 mm of face
#   spacing there.  That is the part of the figure this camera CAN resolve,
#   and `rfade` is what admits it: the surface gradient of the ring
#   coordinate is `sqrt(1 - (u.N)^2)` for `u` the unit radial direction, so
#   `rm/gs` IS the face spacing in metres and the term is faded in against
#   `fw` itself.  Measured, not tasted: the rings appear only where they
#   would occupy more than ~1.15 px and are at full strength past ~2.6.
#
#   WHAT IS LEFT IS STRUCTURE, and the structure of a bench top is BOARDS.
#   Eight of them, 133 mm wide (12 px) with 5 mm joints, each with its
#   own pith depth, pith offset, axis tilt, ring spacing and tone -- so the
#   cathedral figure on one board has nothing to do with its neighbour's,
#   which is the single loudest cue that this is lumber and not a texture.
#   The joints are REAL GEOMETRY (see `geo_bench_top`), not painted lines.
#
# Every art-directable number is a `param` with a range (the doc-88
# human-editability contract).
##################################################

expression_painter
{
	name			expr_top
	# -- the board grid.  THESE TWO MUST MATCH geo_bench_top's part lines:
	#    8 boards of half-width 0.0665625 with 5 mm gaps across the 1.1 m
	#    top, so `board_p` is 0.133125 + 0.005 and `board_z0` is
	#    -(0.55 - 0.0665625).  `sp_top_ring` carries the same two, because
	#    it recomputes the same pith construction (see its comment).
	param			board_p 0.138125 min 0.06 max 0.30 step 0.0005 label "Board pitch (m)"
	param			board_z0 -0.4834375 min -0.55 max 0.0 step 0.0005 label "Centre of the back board (m)"
	param			ring_m 0.0045 min 0.002 max 0.012 step 0.0002 label "Ring spacing, radial (m)"
	param			late_frac 0.28 min 0.05 max 0.6 step 0.01 label "Late-wood fraction of ring"
	param			late_reach 0.44 min 0.05 max 1.0 step 0.02 label "Late-wood field reach (the ramp's dark stops stay reserved)"
	param			base_f 0.58 min 0.2 max 0.9 step 0.01 label "Field centre (early wood)"
	param			tone_b 0.125 min 0.0 max 0.25 step 0.005 label "Board-to-board tone spread"
	param			figure 0.27 min 0.0 max 0.5 step 0.01 label "Coarse grain figure"
	param			joint_d 0.42 min 0.0 max 1.0 step 0.02 label "Dirt and crack at the board joints"
	param			wear_p 0.30 min 0.0 max 0.8 step 0.02 label "Wear where hands and work happen"
	param			knot_d 0.34 min 0.0 max 1.0 step 0.02 label "Knot darkness"
	param			knot_r 0.036 min 0.01 max 0.08 step 0.002 label "Knot radius (m)"
	param			knot_pull 0.010 min 0.0 max 0.04 step 0.001 label "Ring sweep around a knot (m)"
	param			stain_d 0.22 min 0.0 max 0.5 step 0.01 label "Stain darkness (the can ring and the oil patch)"
	param			flange_grime 0.32 min 0.0 max 1.0 step 0.02 label "Grime where something rests on the bench"
	seed			4.0

	# --- DOCTRINE 2, step 1: WHICH BOARD.  `bu` is the board coordinate,
	# integer part = board index, `edge` = 0 at the board's centre line and
	# 1 at its joint.  `cellhash` floors internally, so an INTEGER offset
	# re-indexes the same cell and yields an independent number PER BOARD --
	# the same trick the plank uses per RING, one level up.
	def				bu (Po.z - board_z0)/board_p + 0.5
	def				bk floor(bu)
	def				edge abs(bu - bk - 0.5)*2.0
	def				hA cellhash(bu + 11.0)
	def				hB cellhash(bu + 29.0)
	def				hC cellhash(bu + 47.0)
	def				hD cellhash(bu + 71.0)
	def				hE cellhash(bu + 97.0)
	def				hF cellhash(bu + 131.0)

	# --- step 2: THIS BOARD'S OWN PITH AXIS.  30-115 mm below its face,
	# offset up to 40 mm across it, and tilted 0.9-2.6 degrees out of the
	# board's length.  The tilt is the cathedral: the arch of ring radius R
	# closes where the local pith depth reaches R, and the depth varies
	# along the board precisely because the axis is tilted.  At these tilts
	# an arch spans 150-400 mm of board -- 14 to 37 px, which is why the
	# figure reads at all at this distance.
	def				pdepth 0.050 + 0.140*hA
	def				pithp vec3(0.0, 0.060 - pdepth, board_z0 + bk*board_p + (hB - 0.5)*0.08)
	def				D normalize(vec3(1.0, 0.016 + 0.030*hC, (hD - 0.5)*0.05))
	def				rel Po - pithp
	def				rvec rel - dot(rel, D)*D
	def				r0 max(length(rvec), 0.00002)
	def				rm ring_m*(0.78 + 0.44*hE)

	# --- step 3: the two knots, hand-placed on two different boards
	# (doctrine 4).  The SWEEP is what sells a knot, not the dark disc: the
	# ring coordinate is pulled toward the knot centre, so the crowns near
	# it bend around it.
	def				k1d hypot(Po.x - 0.42, Po.z + 0.2078)
	def				k2d hypot(Po.x + 0.61, Po.z - 0.3463)
	def				kpull knot_pull*(smoothstep(0.11, 0.0, k1d) + smoothstep(0.11, 0.0, k2d))

	# --- step 4: the ring profile.  An ASYMMETRIC SAWTOOTH of ring phase --
	# a flat pale plateau, a narrower dark band, and the abrupt snap back a
	# real ring boundary has.  `cellhash(ph)` gives each RING its own band
	# width and darkness, the way each growing season does.
	#
	# THERE IS NO RING-EDGE WARP HERE, and the plank has one.  It was
	# authored (17 mm across the grain x 170 mm along, held under a quarter
	# ring, exactly the plank's), and it was DELETED rather than left in:
	# `fbm` fades the octaves the pixel footprint cannot resolve, and 17 mm
	# against a 10.7 mm footprint is inside that fade, so the term returned
	# essentially zero over the whole top.  A dead term in a field is worse
	# than no term -- it reads as authored intent and is not.
	def				ph (r0 - kpull)/rm
	def				f ph - floor(ph)
	def				lf late_frac*(0.72 + 0.56*cellhash(ph))
	def				late smoothstep(1.0 - lf, 1.0 - 0.30*lf, f)*(0.72 + 0.56*cellhash(ph + 13.0))
	def				lateb late_reach*min(late, 1.15)

	# --- step 5: THE FOOTPRINT GATE.  `gs` is the magnitude of the ring
	# coordinate's gradient PROJECTED INTO THE SURFACE, so `rm/gs` is the
	# ring spacing a pixel actually straddles.  See this chunk's header.
	def				un rvec/r0
	def				unN dot(un, N)
	def				gs sqrt(max(1.0 - unN*unN, 0.0004))
	def				rfade smoothstep(1.15*fw, 2.6*fw, rm/gs)

	# --- step 6: the coarse figure, at 38 mm across x 145 mm along (3.5 px
	# x 13 px).  It is legitimate at this amplitude precisely BECAUSE it is
	# coarser than the ring spacing, so it moves neighbouring rings together
	# instead of scrambling them -- and it is the only wood term that
	# survives everywhere on this top, the rings having been faded out
	# between the crowns.
	def				fig fbm(vec3(Po.x*3.5 + bk*3.7, Po.y*13.0, Po.z*13.0 + bk*8.3) + vec3(seed*5.3, 2.0, 9.0), 2, 0.55, 2.0)
	# ... and the SAME undulation WITHOUT the per-board break, for channel G.
	def				figc fbm(vec3(Po.x*3.5, Po.y*13.0, Po.z*13.0) + vec3(seed*5.3, 2.0, 9.0), 2, 0.55, 2.0)

	# --- DOCTRINE 3: PROCESS TIED TO GEOMETRY.
	#
	# `convexity(0.010)` is the board arrises -- every joint lip and the
	# whole front edge.  Its radius is a FRACTION of this object's bbox
	# diagonal (3.20 m), so 0.010 is 32 mm, and a 5 mm-rounded arris reads
	# about 0.5 there (0.5 IS a 90-degree arris, on any object at any
	# scale).  The lips are where a forearm and a workpiece actually rub.
	#
	# `convexity` and the joint band PARTITION the joint: the polish sits ON
	# the lip, the dirt in the 22 mm beside it, and neither needs a
	# threshold to keep it out of the other.  `occlusion()` is deliberately
	# NOT used for the joint -- a point on the top face beside an 8 mm slot
	# has a FULLY OPEN hemisphere and reads occlusion 1, correctly.  The
	# crack is dark because it is a real crack, and the dirt beside it is
	# keyed on the analytic distance to the board edge, which is a fact this
	# field already knows.
	def				arris convexity(0.010)
	def				upface smoothstep(0.10, 0.50, N.y)
	def				jb fbm(vec3(Po.x*9.0, Po.y*9.0, Po.z*9.0) + vec3(seed*2.7, 5.0, 1.0), 2, 0.5, 2.0)
	def				inner smoothstep(0.55, 0.50, abs(Po.z))
	def				crack smoothstep(0.76, 0.86, edge)*inner*upface
	def				grime smoothstep(0.55, 0.84, edge)*clamp(0.50 + 0.95*jb, 0, 1)*inner*upface
	def				joint clamp(0.75*crack + 0.55*grime, 0, 1)

	# Wear is WHERE THE WORK IS: a 190 mm band along the front edge where a
	# body leans, a 460 mm halo around the vise where everything is clamped,
	# hammered and filed, and the arrises.  Broken up at 250 mm so it is a
	# region rather than a drawn shape.
	def				frontw smoothstep(0.34, 0.53, Po.z)*upface
	def				visew smoothstep(0.46, 0.12, hypot(Po.x - 1.15, Po.z + 0.02))
	def				wearb fbm(vec3(Po.x*4.0, Po.y*4.0, Po.z*4.0) + vec3(seed*7.7, 3.0, 6.0), 2, 0.5, 2.0)
	def				wearf clamp((0.50*frontw + 0.80*visew + 1.30*arris)*(0.80 + 1.10*wearb), 0, 1)

	# --- THE FLANGE GRIME, and the retraction of this file's own claim that
	# it could not be had.  The header used to say grime under the vise's
	# flange "is out of reach ... occlusion() is self-occlusion only".  That
	# was true of every signal this scene had; it is not true any more.
	# `proximity(r)` measures the distance from this hit to the nearest
	# surface of any OTHER object -- 1 at touching, 0 at `r`.
	#
	# ITS RADIUS IS A WORLD LENGTH, unlike `occlusion`'s per-object fraction:
	# `proximity(0.02)` is 20 mm, on this bench or any other.  20 mm is set
	# from the flange's own geometry.  The flange is a `roundbox` with
	# half-extents (0.195, 0.021, 0.172) m and an 18 mm rounding, so its flat
	# underside stops 18 mm inside the nominal edge and the last 18 mm is a
	# quarter-round lifting away from the bench.  Measured on the bench top
	# with the query itself, outward from that touchdown line at the middle
	# of each of the four sides: 0.042 mm of separation at the line, 0.56-0.97
	# mm at 5 mm out, 8.7-9.3 mm at 20 mm and 20.6-21.9 mm at 35 mm -- so a
	# 20 mm radius saturates under the lip, is a little past half way out at
	# 20 mm, and has died before the grime could reach clean bench.
	#
	# AND THE RADIUS HAS A CEILING HERE, which is why the BROAD wear halo
	# above is hand-placed rather than just being this term at 0.3 m: the
	# legs' tops stand 10 mm INSIDE the top's underside, 120 mm below its
	# face, so any radius past ~0.11 m paints a grime blob on the bench over
	# each leg.  20 mm is well clear of that.
	#
	# IT IS SUBTRACTIVE, because this ramp runs dark -> pale: grime darkens,
	# so it takes the field down.  `fgb` breaks it up at ~25 mm so the ring
	# hugging the flange is patchy rather than a drawn outline.  NOTE the
	# 0.85 centre on that multiplier: `fbm` is SIGNED and zero-mean here, so
	# a multiplier written `0.45 + 1.35*fgb` would average 0.45, not 0.9.
	def				contact proximity(0.02)
	def				fgb fbm(vec3(P.x*40.0, P.y*40.0, P.z*40.0) + vec3(seed*1.9, 8.0, 2.0), 3, 0.5, 2.0)
	def				flange clamp(contact*(0.85 + 1.5*fgb), 0, 1)

	# --- DOCTRINE 4: RARE EVENTS.  Two of them, both hand-placed, both at
	# LOW contrast: the ring a wet tin left (144 mm across, a 2 px band) and
	# one 420 mm patch where oil soaked in.  Not a scatter of blotches --
	# the whole point is that most of this top has nothing on it.
	def				canring smoothstep(0.020, 0.004, abs(hypot(Po.x + 0.34, Po.z - 0.16) - 0.072))
	def				oilb fbm(vec3(Po.x*8.0, Po.y*8.0, Po.z*8.0) + vec3(seed*4.4, 7.0, 2.0), 2, 0.5, 2.0)
	def				oilp smoothstep(0.21, 0.05, hypot(Po.x - 0.72, Po.z + 0.12))*clamp(0.45 + 1.5*oilb, 0, 1)
	def				knots knot_d*(smoothstep(knot_r, knot_r*0.4, k1d) + smoothstep(knot_r, knot_r*0.4, k2d))

	# --- THE WOOD, which is channel G: board tone, coarse figure, ring crowns.
	def				wood clamp(base_f + tone_b*(hF - 0.5)*2.0 + figure*fig - lateb*rfade, 0, 1)
	def				shape clamp(base_f + figure*figc, 0, 1)

	# --- THE SUM, AND WHY IT IS A vec3.
	#
	# `late_reach` and `tone_b` are ENFORCED reservations, not claims in a
	# comment: a ramp cannot reserve a range the field is free to write into,
	# so the late-wood term is bounded where it is summed.
	#
	# R IS WHAT THE SURFACE LOOKS LIKE; G IS WHAT THE SURFACE IS SHAPED LIKE.
	# This is still ONE field with one evaluation -- the ramp and the
	# roughness bridge read `channel R`, `relief_modifier`'s bridge reads
	# `channel G` -- but the two channels stop at different points in the same
	# sum.  R is everything: board tone, the per-board figure, the ring crowns
	# the footprint gate admits, and every PROCESS term -- wear, joint dirt,
	# flange grime, knots, stains.  G (`shape`) is the CONTINUOUS coarse
	# undulation and nothing else.
	#
	# The split is not tidiness, it is a MEASUREMENT.  Binding the whole field
	# raw to relief -- which is what the plank does, and what this file's
	# relief comment used to argue for -- put 591 near-black pixels per 10000
	# on this top, all of them on the joint lines and the near arris.  The
	# mechanism is the one `max_slope` exists to manage, hit from the other
	# side: the joint dirt is a 0.28 step across ~10 mm, a field gradient of
	# 28 per metre, which at `scale 0.40` is a slope of 11 against a clamp of
	# 0.18 -- so the clamp binds at FULL tilt over every joint, and on a
	# surface already at 78 degrees of incidence a full-tilt away-facing
	# normal is past the geometric horizon and shades BLACK.  A process term
	# has no business having a height at all; giving it one is what made the
	# lines black.
	#
	# G ALSO STOPS BEFORE THE TWO PER-BOARD TERMS -- `tone_b` and the
	# per-board domain offset in `fig` -- for the same reason one level down.
	# The relief stencil is `fw`/2 = 5.4 mm wide and the joint is 5 mm, so
	# within a centimetre of every joint the four taps straddle a change of
	# `bk` and difference a step that is not a height.  `figc` is that same
	# undulation WITHOUT the per-board break.  Measured: adding the per-board
	# offset to the relief channel alone cost 270 near-black per 10000.
	expr			vec3(clamp(wood + wear_p*wearf - joint_d*joint - flange_grime*flange - knots - stain_d*(0.62*canring + 0.85*oilp), 0, 1), shape, 0.0)
}

# CONSUMER 1 -- COLOUR.  Aged softwood: joint dirt and knot core at the
# bottom, late wood, the early-wood PLATEAU, and burnished wear at the top.
#
# The plateau is held nearly flat from 0.52 to 0.88 on purpose: a smooth
# colour gradient across each ring gives every ring the tonal signature of
# a shaded cylinder and the board turns into corrugated sheet.
#
# Plateau-to-late-wood is 0.273 -> 0.112 in luminance, 2.4:1.  Real pine is
# nearer 2.5:1; the 18:1 an eager ramp reaches for is itself a "generated"
# tell and it drowns the relief, which is the cue that actually reads as
# carved.  The two darkest stops are RESERVED for the knots, the joint dirt
# and the grime -- and `expr_top`'s sum is what enforces that reservation.
#
# The old ramp here ran to 0.78 0.55 0.30, an R:B of 2.6 that rendered as
# cartoon orange.  1.7 is what aged, dust-greyed softwood actually is.
ramp_painter
{
	name			ramp_top
	input			expr_top
	channel			R
	interpolation	smooth
	stop			0.00  0.055 0.043 0.032
	stop			0.14  0.088 0.068 0.048
	stop			0.32  0.135 0.100 0.065
	stop			0.48  0.205 0.152 0.099
	stop			0.64  0.290 0.219 0.146
	stop			0.80  0.375 0.290 0.198
	stop			1.00  0.460 0.372 0.272
	color_space		Rec709RGB_Linear
}

# CONSUMER 2 -- the PHYSICAL SCALAR, off the SAME field.  The bridge's
# scale is NEGATIVE: the field's high end is burnished wear, which is the
# SMOOTH end, and its low end is dirt and grime, which is the matte one.
# 0.66 at the dirt down to 0.24 where a forearm has polished it.
scalar_painter
{
	name			sp_top_rough
	painter			expr_top
	channel			R
	scale			-0.42
	bias			0.66
}

# CONSUMER 2b -- THE RINGS, WHICH ONLY ROUGHNESS CAN CARRY.  This is the
# sub-pixel half of the ring field: the same asymmetric sawtooth as
# `expr_top`'s, with NO footprint gate, zero-meaned and bridged into
# roughness alone.  At 4.5 mm against a 10.7 mm pixel it can carry no shape
# and would read as exactly the dirty noise that gives a render away -- but
# it can still modulate gloss, and a half-pixel band of duller sheen is
# what late wood looks like from three and a half metres.
#
# It recomputes the pith construction rather than bridging `expr_top`
# because a bridge can only scale a field, and what is wanted here is one
# TERM of it without the gate the colour pipe needs.
scalar_painter
{
	name			sp_top_ring
	param			ring_m 0.0045 min 0.002 max 0.012 step 0.0002 label "Ring spacing, radial (m)"
	param			late_frac 0.28 min 0.05 max 0.6 step 0.01 label "Late-wood fraction of ring"
	param			ring_gloss 0.11 min 0.0 max 0.3 step 0.005 label "Ring gloss modulation"
	param			board_p 0.138125 min 0.06 max 0.30 step 0.0005 label "Board pitch (m)"
	param			board_z0 -0.4834375 min -0.55 max 0.0 step 0.0005 label "Centre of the back board (m)"
	seed			4.0
	def				bu (Po.z - board_z0)/board_p + 0.5
	def				bk floor(bu)
	def				pdepth 0.050 + 0.140*cellhash(bu + 11.0)
	def				pithp vec3(0.0, 0.060 - pdepth, board_z0 + bk*board_p + (cellhash(bu + 29.0) - 0.5)*0.08)
	def				D normalize(vec3(1.0, 0.016 + 0.030*cellhash(bu + 47.0), (cellhash(bu + 71.0) - 0.5)*0.05))
	def				rel Po - pithp
	def				rvec rel - dot(rel, D)*D
	def				r0 max(length(rvec), 0.00002)
	def				rm ring_m*(0.78 + 0.44*cellhash(bu + 97.0))
	def				ph r0/rm
	def				f ph - floor(ph)
	def				lf late_frac*(0.72 + 0.56*cellhash(ph))
	expression		ring_gloss*(smoothstep(1.0 - lf, 1.0 - 0.30*lf, f) - 0.5)
}

# CONSUMER 2c -- the flange grime's roughness, which needs its own bridge
# for a reason worth writing down.  `sp_top_rough` has a NEGATIVE scale, and
# the grime term SUBTRACTS from the field (it darkens), so through that
# bridge it would come out SMOOTHER -- a polished ring around the vise, the
# opposite of dirt.  One bridge cannot give one field two signs, so the
# contact term gets its own additive scalar and the sum is what the material
# binds.  `contact` is still one definition; it is the SIGN of the bridge
# that forks.
#
# +0.13 at a saturated seam, against the 0.24-0.66 the wood occupies.  The
# breakup is the SAME `fgb` construction as the colour term's, so the dull
# patches and the dark patches coincide -- one deposit of dirt, not two
# independent noises.
scalar_painter
{
	name			sp_flange_rough
	param			flange_rough 0.13 min 0.0 max 0.4 step 0.01 label "Flange-grime roughening"
	seed			4.0
	def				contact proximity(0.02)
	def				fgb fbm(vec3(P.x*40.0, P.y*40.0, P.z*40.0) + vec3(seed*1.9, 8.0, 2.0), 3, 0.5, 2.0)
	expression		flange_rough*clamp(contact*(0.85 + 1.5*fgb), 0, 1)
}

scalar_painter
{
	name			sp_top_rough_ring
	add				sp_top_rough sp_top_ring
	weight_a		1.0
	weight_b		1.0
}

scalar_painter
{
	name			sp_wood_rough
	add				sp_top_rough_ring sp_flange_rough
	weight_a		1.0
	weight_b		1.0
}

# CONSUMER 3 -- RELIEF (docs/RELIEF_MODIFIER_DESIGN.md Phase 5): the SAME
# field, read through its STRUCTURAL channel (`channel G`) and bridged raw --
# no scale/bias, because `relief_modifier`'s own `scale` below is the
# independent height-amplitude multiplier.  `ramp_top` maps field=0 -> dirt
# and field=1 -> burnished pale, so the field rises with "paleness" and a
# POSITIVE scale leaves the dark end at the local floor: dark reads SUNK,
# which is the sign this weathered bench wants.
#
# WHY G AND NOT R -- and it took the four rounds recorded in the header.
# Binding the WHOLE field, process terms included (which is what the plank
# does), put 591 near-black pixels per 10000 on this top.  A process term
# has no business having a height, and the joint dirt in particular is a
# 0.28 step across ~10 mm: a field gradient of 28 per metre, which at
# `scale 0.40` is a slope of 11 against a clamp of 0.18 -- so the clamp
# binds at FULL tilt over every joint, and on a surface at 78 degrees of
# incidence a full-tilt away-facing normal is past the geometric horizon
# and shades BLACK.  G stops before the process terms, and it also stops
# before the two PER-BOARD terms (`tone_b` and the per-board domain offset
# in `fig`), because `step 0` resolves to `fw`/2 = 5.4 mm and the 4-tap
# stencil straddles the 5 mm joint -- so a per-board step is differenced as
# a cliff every time the stencil crosses one.  What is left in G, and what
# relief here actually is, is the CONTINUOUS coarse undulation: `figc`.
#
# WHAT RELIEF CANNOT DO AT THIS FOOTPRINT: the rings.  `step 0` resolves to
# `fw`/2 = 5.4 mm, so a 4.5 mm ring is differenced across a stencil wider
# than itself and comes back as nothing.  The relief that DOES read is the
# coarse figure at 77 x 285 mm, which is the honest answer for a surface
# seen from 3.5 m -- and the joints, which are not relief at all but real
# geometry.
#
# AMPLITUDE + CLAMP, swept on the SHIPPED field (640x480, the 231-column
# top-face band between the tray and the vise; hf-RMS is the row-wise
# second-difference RMS across the grain):
#
#     scale / max_slope     near-black per 10000     hf-RMS
#        0.40 / 0.30               265                4.31
#        0.25 / 0.30                53                3.45
#        0.40 / 0.18                 0                2.99   <- shipped
#        0.55 / 0.13                 0                2.90
#
# `max_slope` bounds the TILT rather than the amplitude: it rescales the
# gradient to that slope with its DIRECTION PRESERVED, so `scale` can be
# raised for legibility while the clamp holds the peaks -- which is why the
# shipped pair raises `scale` to 0.40 and tightens the clamp instead of
# dialling `scale` down (that would flatten the shallow parts of the field
# along with the steep ones).  Past 0.40/0.18 the clamp binds so widely that
# more `scale` buys nothing: 0.55/0.13 is measurably WORSE on detail.
#
# 0.18 is far below the 0.5-1.0 band the skill doc recommends generally, and
# below the 0.30 this file used before the top was rebuilt.  The reason is
# geometric: this top is a large horizontal plane seen from 14 degrees above
# it, and the single most grazing surface in the frame is the eased front
# arris, which is where the last black pixels lived at 0.30.  atan(0.18) is
# 10.2 degrees of tilt budget; the arris cannot afford more.
#
# THE KEY LIGHT WAS LEFT ALONE, having been tested.  Lowering it from 42 to
# 24 degrees elevation does NOT help the grain read: fine-detail contrast
# goes slightly DOWN, the top dims ~7%, and near-black pixels rise 35%.  The
# reason is the same grazing-budget geometry as the `max_slope` clamp
# itself, applied to the LIGHT instead of the camera: a clamp of `m` caps
# the tilt at atan(m), so an away-facing tilt can point the shading normal
# that far past the light's own direction before N'.L goes non-positive.
# At the 42-degree key and the 0.4 clamp that test used, the margin is
# 42-21.8 = 20.2 degrees; at a 24-degree key it is 2.2, so far more of the
# field's tilt distribution crosses that horizon and gets gated black.
#
# `domain surface` -- the boards are an `sdf_geometry`, which has no UVs.
scalar_painter
{
	name			sp_wood_relief
	painter			expr_top
	channel			G
}

relief_modifier
{
	name			relief_wood
	height			sp_wood_relief
	domain			surface
	scale			0.40
	step			0
	max_slope		0.18
}

##################################################
# Painters -- THE LEGS.  The same wood, at their own footprint, plain.
#
# A leg is ONE post, not eight boards, and its grain runs VERTICALLY -- so
# it cannot share the top's field, whose whole construction is "which board
# am I on, and where is that board's pith".  It gets the same asymmetric
# sawtooth about a pith axis and the same footprint gate, with the axis
# along Y, no joints, no stains and no wear.
#
# ITS FOOTPRINT IS DIFFERENT AND THAT IS THE POINT.  The legs are seen
# nearly face-on rather than at 14 degrees, so `fw` there is about 5.8 mm
# against the top's 10.7 -- roughly twice the resolution, and the gate
# admits the rings over correspondingly more of the surface.  Nothing was
# hand-tuned for that; `rfade` reads `fw` and follows.
#
# The four legs differ by an object-space id (`legid`) built from the leg's
# own world position, floored so it is constant over each leg: four posts
# out of one material, each with its own pith and tone.
##################################################

expression_painter
{
	name			expr_leg
	param			ring_m 0.0045 min 0.002 max 0.012 step 0.0002 label "Ring spacing, radial (m)"
	param			late_frac 0.28 min 0.05 max 0.6 step 0.01 label "Late-wood fraction of ring"
	param			late_reach 0.22 min 0.05 max 1.0 step 0.02 label "Late-wood field reach"
	param			base_f 0.62 min 0.2 max 0.9 step 0.01 label "Field centre (early wood)"
	param			tone_b 0.070 min 0.0 max 0.25 step 0.005 label "Leg-to-leg tone spread"
	param			figure 0.34 min 0.0 max 0.5 step 0.01 label "Coarse grain figure"
	param			foot_d 0.26 min 0.0 max 0.8 step 0.02 label "Dirt at the foot and under the apron"
	seed			9.0

	# -- which leg.  `floor(P.x + 4)` is constant over a 120 mm post at
	# x = +-1.35 and `floor(P.z + 4)` over one at z = +-0.45, so this is a
	# stable per-object id without a per-leg material.
	def				legid floor(P.x + 4.0) + 5.0*floor(P.z + 4.0)
	def				gA cellhash(legid + 3.0)
	def				gB cellhash(legid + 17.0)
	def				gC cellhash(legid + 37.0)
	def				gD cellhash(legid + 59.0)

	# -- the pith axis runs UP the post, 40-110 mm outside its 120 mm
	# section, tilted about a degree so the rings drift along the leg.
	def				poff 0.100 + 0.070*gA
	def				pang 6.2831853*gB
	def				pithp vec3(poff*cos(pang), 0.0, poff*sin(pang))
	def				D normalize(vec3((gC - 0.5)*0.04, 1.0, (gD - 0.5)*0.04))
	def				rel Po - pithp
	def				rvec rel - dot(rel, D)*D
	def				r0 max(length(rvec), 0.00002)
	def				rm ring_m*(0.78 + 0.44*gC)

	def				ph r0/rm
	def				f ph - floor(ph)
	def				lf late_frac*(0.72 + 0.56*cellhash(ph))
	def				late smoothstep(1.0 - lf, 1.0 - 0.30*lf, f)*(0.72 + 0.56*cellhash(ph + 13.0))
	def				lateb late_reach*min(late, 1.15)

	# -- the same footprint gate as the top's, reading the legs' own `fw`.
	def				un rvec/r0
	def				unN dot(un, N)
	def				gs sqrt(max(1.0 - unN*unN, 0.0004))
	def				rfade smoothstep(1.15*fw, 2.6*fw, rm/gs)

	def				fig fbm(vec3(Po.x*13.0, Po.y*3.5, Po.z*13.0) + vec3(9.0, seed*5.3, 2.0), 2, 0.55, 2.0)

	# -- the one process term.  `proximity(0.02)` is the same cross-object
	# signal the top uses, and on a leg it finds BOTH seams for free: the
	# floor the foot stands on and the underside of the top the leg is
	# housed into.  The FLOOR side of the lower seam stays unpainted -- it
	# is a deliberately flat `uniformcolor_painter`, a supporting surface
	# out of the depth of interest.
	def				seam proximity(0.02)
	def				sb fbm(vec3(P.x*40.0, P.y*40.0, P.z*40.0) + vec3(seed*1.9, 8.0, 2.0), 3, 0.5, 2.0)

	def				wood clamp(base_f + tone_b*(gD - 0.5)*2.0 + figure*fig - lateb*rfade, 0, 1)
	expr			vec3(clamp(wood - foot_d*clamp(seam*(0.85 + 1.5*sb), 0, 1), 0, 1), wood, 0.0)
}

ramp_painter
{
	name			ramp_leg
	input			expr_leg
	channel			R
	interpolation	smooth
	stop			0.00  0.055 0.043 0.032
	stop			0.14  0.088 0.068 0.048
	stop			0.32  0.135 0.100 0.065
	stop			0.48  0.205 0.152 0.099
	stop			0.64  0.290 0.219 0.146
	stop			0.80  0.375 0.290 0.198
	stop			1.00  0.460 0.372 0.272
	color_space		Rec709RGB_Linear
}

scalar_painter
{
	name			sp_leg_rough
	painter			expr_leg
	channel			R
	scale			-0.30
	bias			0.62
}

scalar_painter
{
	name			sp_leg_relief
	painter			expr_leg
	channel			G
}

# The legs are seen nearly FACE-ON, not at 14 degrees like the top, so the
# grazing-budget argument that pins the top at `max_slope 0.30` does not
# apply to them -- their geometric horizon is far away and they can carry
# the 0.5-1.0 the skill doc recommends generally.  0.45 is the value that
# reads without any of them showing a black tilt.
relief_modifier
{
	name			relief_leg
	height			sp_leg_relief
	domain			surface
	scale			0.25
	step			0
	max_slope		0.45
}

uniformcolor_painter
{
	name			pnt_wood_spec
	color			0.4 0.35 0.28
}

##################################################
# Painters -- the vise: ONE cast-iron field, four consumers
##################################################

# THE FIELD.  Sizes come from the MEASURED footprint over the vise
# (`fw` p50 = 7.95 mm; see the geometry comment below), so the ordering
# is: casting undulation at 6 px, chips at 4-7 px, and NOTHING finer in
# colour or relief -- a real casting's 1-3 mm pitting is a fifth of a
# pixel here and can only read as dirt, so it goes to roughness alone
# (`sp_vise_grain`).
#
# The axis the field runs along is a WEATHERING axis, not a brightness
# one: 0 = bare machined steel, ~0.30 = intact paint, ~0.62 = the cast
# iron a chip exposes, 1.0 = rust bloom.  Everything that moves a point
# along it is either a sized surface process or a question the geometry
# answers about itself.
expression_painter
{
	name			expr_vise
	param			cast_freq 30.0 min 6 max 90 step 1 label "Casting undulation frequency"
	param			patch_freq 15.0 min 4 max 50 step 1 label "Casting-scale patch frequency"
	param			chip_freq 32.0 min 8 max 90 step 1 label "Paint-chip field frequency"
	param			chip_cov 0.16 min -0.3 max 0.6 step 0.02 label "Paint-chip threshold (lower = more bare iron)"
	param			paint_w 0.42 min 0.0 max 0.8 step 0.02 label "Field value of intact paint"
	param			iron_w 0.66 min 0.3 max 0.9 step 0.02 label "Field value of bare cast iron"
	param			steel_w 0.06 min 0.0 max 0.3 step 0.01 label "Field value of machined steel"
	param			rust_crev 0.55 min 0.0 max 1.5 step 0.05 label "Rust in crevices"
	param			grime_crev 0.16 min 0.0 max 0.6 step 0.02 label "Grime darkening the paint in crevices"
	param			rust_down 0.40 min 0.0 max 1.5 step 0.05 label "Rust on the underside"
	seed			19.0

	# -- geometry signals (doctrine 3).  Every threshold here was MEASURED
	# on this object, not guessed: a probe render of each signal, divided
	# by a flat-albedo control, gave curv p25/p50/p75/p90 = 0 / 10.4 /
	# 27.7 / 32.9 and occlusion(0.09) p2/p10/p25/p50 = 0.43 / 0.70 / 0.87
	# / 0.97.  `curv` is normalised by the object's bbox diagonal (0.835 m
	# here: the geometry's bbox is 0.4415 x 0.3637 x 0.6087 m), so a flat cast face reads 0, the screw and handle barrels
	# read ~17, and only the drawn arrises of the castings reach 30+ --
	# which is why `arris` starts at 18 and not anywhere near 1.
	def				occ occlusion(0.09)
	def				crev smoothstep(0.84, 0.50, occ)
	def				down clamp(-N.y, 0, 1)
	def				arris smoothstep(18.0, 34.0, curv)

	# -- the machined-part mask, in OBJECT space (`Po`), because "is this
	# face machined or as-cast" is a fact about the PART, not about a
	# noise field, and a vise's whole visual argument is the contrast
	# between the two.  Three bands: the two jaw pads and the slot
	# between them; the slide bar where it shows between the castings;
	# and everything forward of the movable jaw (screw, collar, handle).
	#
	# Every transition below is placed ON a real geometric edge -- the
	# pad's own boundary at |Po.z - 0.0295| = 0.0445, its lower face at
	# Po.y = 0.171, the bar's flank at |Po.x| = 0.054, the jaw casting's
	# front at Po.z = 0.173.  That is not decoration: `relief_vise` reads
	# the same field, so a mask edge is a height step, and a height step
	# is only invisible when it lands where the silhouette already has a
	# crease.
	def				padband smoothstep(0.055, 0.047, abs(Po.z - 0.0295)) * smoothstep(0.163, 0.170, Po.y) * smoothstep(0.124, 0.117, abs(Po.x))
	def				barband smoothstep(0.060, 0.050, abs(Po.x)) * smoothstep(0.106, 0.114, Po.y) * smoothstep(0.194, 0.186, Po.y) * smoothstep(0.170, 0.158, Po.z)
	def				frontband smoothstep(0.174, 0.182, Po.z)
	def				mach clamp(padband + barband + frontband, 0, 1)

	# -- surface processes, sized to the footprint.
	# 33 mm sand-casting undulation: 6 px, so it carries SHAPE and is the
	# only term `relief_vise` really renders.
	def				und fbm(P*cast_freq + vec3(seed, seed*1.7, seed*2.3), 2, 0.5, 2.0)
	# 67 mm casting-scale drift: 12 px, coarser than the undulation, so it
	# moves neighbouring ripples together instead of scrambling them.
	def				patch smoothstep(-0.12, 0.24, fbm(P*patch_freq + vec3(3.0, 8.0, 1.0), 3, 0.5, 2.0))

	# -- the paint layer.  Paint does not chip at random: it comes off the
	# ARRISES first, off the casting's high spots next, and it survives in
	# the broad flat panels.  So the chip field is `arris` and `und` added
	# to a 31 mm noise and thresholded HARD (0.05 wide) -- a chip edge is
	# crisp, and a soft one reads as a stain.
	def				chipf fbm(P*chip_freq + vec3(11.0, 2.0, 5.0), 2, 0.55, 2.1)
	def				runs fbm(vec3(P.x*88.0, P.y*11.0, P.z*88.0) + vec3(6.0, 1.0, 3.0), 2, 0.5, 2.0)
	def				bare smoothstep(chip_cov, chip_cov + 0.05, 1.4*chipf + 0.18*arris + 0.30*und + 0.35*runs)

	# -- rust.  Concentrated where water sits and cannot dry: the crevices
	# `occlusion()` finds (jaw-to-body fillet, under the flange lip,
	# around the bolt heads) and the downward faces `-N.y` finds.  It only
	# exists where the paint is already gone, which is what `* bare` says.
	def				rust clamp(0.15 + rust_crev*crev + rust_down*down + 0.45*patch + 0.55*runs, 0, 1)

	# -- the two surfaces, mixed by the part mask.  `iron_w` and the rust
	# reach are held so the CAST branch tops out just under 1.0 before the
	# undulation is added: a ramp cannot reserve a range the field is free
	# to write into.
	def				grime grime_crev*crev*(1.0 - bare)
	def				cast paint_w - grime + (iron_w - paint_w)*bare + (0.94 - iron_w)*bare*rust + 0.26*und
	def				steel steel_w + 0.09*rust + 0.05*und
	expr			clamp(mix(cast, steel, mach), 0, 1)
}

# CONSUMER 1 -- colour.  Machined steel -> dulled steel -> a flat plateau
# of intact paint -> the cast iron a chip exposes -> rust.  The paint
# occupies 0.26..0.55 as a near-constant colour on purpose (doctrine: a
# smooth gradient across a plateau reads as a row of rods), and the
# paint-to-iron step at 0.55 -> 0.62 is deliberately tight because a
# chip boundary is an edge, not a fade.
ramp_painter
{
	name			ramp_vise
	input			expr_vise
	channel			R
	interpolation	smooth
	stop			0.00  0.185 0.195 0.210
	stop			0.11  0.118 0.124 0.134
	stop			0.20  0.032 0.039 0.050
	stop			0.32  0.046 0.058 0.081
	stop			0.46  0.078 0.099 0.136
	stop			0.60  0.112 0.140 0.186
	stop			0.66  0.042 0.040 0.036
	stop			0.76  0.082 0.070 0.056
	stop			0.87  0.250 0.118 0.050
	stop			1.00  0.365 0.185 0.088
	color_space		Rec709RGB_Linear
}

# CONSUMER 2 -- roughness, linear off the same field: machined steel 0.10,
# paint 0.26, bare iron 0.47, rust 0.72.
scalar_painter
{
	name			sp_vise_rough_base
	painter			expr_vise
	channel			R
	scale			0.66
	bias			0.06
}

# ... plus the ONE term that is deliberately NOT in the field: sand-casting
# grain at 4.5 mm.  That is 0.8 of a pixel here, so by doctrine 1 it can
# carry neither colour nor shape -- but a microfacet distribution is
# exactly where sub-pixel structure belongs, and breaking the specular up
# per-pixel is most of what stops a GGX surface reading as moulded plastic.
scalar_painter
{
	name			sp_vise_grain
	param			grain_freq 220.0 min 60 max 600 step 10 label "Sand-cast grain frequency"
	def				g fbm(P*grain_freq + vec3(4.0, 7.0, 1.0), 2, 0.5, 2.0)
	expression		0.075*g
}

scalar_painter
{
	name			sp_vise_rough
	add				sp_vise_rough_base sp_vise_grain
	weight_a		1.0
	weight_b		1.0
}

# CONSUMER 3 -- relief.  NEGATIVE scale: the field's high end is rust and
# the hollows it eats, and a hollow sinks.  `scale` is set from the
# undulation's own geometry rather than swept blind -- a 33 mm ripple with
# about 0.8 mm of amplitude wants a slope near 0.16, and the undulation
# term moves the field by about +-0.045, so 0.16/(0.045/0.033) = 0.05.
# `max_slope` then bounds what the chip edges do with the same scale (the
# field steps ~0.3 across ~10 mm there, which is a slope of 30 before the
# clamp); 0.30 is the same knee the bench top's own sweep found, and for
# the same reason -- most of this scene is seen at a grazing angle, which
# brings the geometric horizon in close.  `step 0` resolves to fw/2 ~ 4 mm,
# an eighth of the undulation's wavelength, so the ripple is differenced
# well inside its own feature.
scalar_painter
{
	name			sp_vise_relief
	painter			expr_vise
	channel			R
}

relief_modifier
{
	name			relief_vise
	height			sp_vise_relief
	domain			surface
	scale			-0.16
	step			0
	max_slope		0.45
}

# CONSUMER 4 -- the FRESNEL.  The same field decides whether a point is
# metal or not, which no amount of colour can fake: bare machined steel is
# a conductor (ior 2.9, k 3.3, F0 ~ 0.7), while paint and rust are
# dielectrics (ior ~1.6, k ~0, F0 ~ 0.05).  Both slots are physical
# scalars, so both are `scalar_painter` bridges and neither goes anywhere
# near a colourspace conversion.  A chip is left on the dielectric side on
# purpose: exposed iron on a bench vise is oxide within days, not mirror.
scalar_painter
{
	name			sp_vise_ior
	painter			expr_vise
	channel			R
	scale			-1.30
	bias			2.90
}

scalar_painter
{
	name			sp_vise_extinction
	painter			expr_vise
	channel			R
	scale			-3.30
	bias			3.45
}

uniformcolor_painter
{
	name			pnt_vise_spec
	color			0.56 0.57 0.60
	colorspace		Rec709RGB_Linear
}

##################################################
# Painters -- tool tray: stochastic_tile_painter de-tiling a checker source
##################################################

uniformcolor_painter
{
	name			pnt_plate_dark
	color			0.08 0.08 0.09
}

uniformcolor_painter
{
	name			pnt_plate_bright
	color			0.75 0.75 0.78
}

# No tileable photo/scan asset (bark/plaster/rust) exists in-repo, so this
# substitutes a procedural checker source to exercise the de-tiling
# MECHANISM.  stochastic_tile_painter's intended use is a small tiling
# photo/scan, per the skill -- this is not that, just a stand-in source.
checker_painter
{
	name			plate_checker_src
	colora			pnt_plate_dark
	colorb			pnt_plate_bright
	size			0.09
}

stochastic_tile_painter
{
	name			plate_stochastic
	source			plate_checker_src
	tile_scale		10.0
	seed			3
	mean			0.42 0.42 0.43
	blend_gamma		7.0
	color_space		Rec709RGB_Linear
}

uniformcolor_painter
{
	name			pnt_tray_spec
	color			0.5 0.5 0.5
}

##################################################
# Painters -- floor
##################################################

uniformcolor_painter
{
	name			pnt_floor
	color			0.22 0.22 0.24
}

##################################################
# Materials
##################################################

ggx_material
{
	name			mat_wood_top
	rd				ramp_top
	rs				pnt_wood_spec
	alphax			sp_wood_rough
	alphay			sp_wood_rough
	ior				1.5
	extinction		0.0
}

ggx_material
{
	name			mat_wood_leg
	rd				ramp_leg
	rs				pnt_wood_spec
	alphax			sp_leg_rough
	alphay			sp_leg_rough
	ior				1.5
	extinction		0.0
}

ggx_material
{
	name			mat_vise
	rd				ramp_vise
	rs				pnt_vise_spec
	alphax			sp_vise_rough
	alphay			sp_vise_rough
	ior				sp_vise_ior
	extinction		sp_vise_extinction
}

ggx_material
{
	name			mat_tray
	rd				plate_stochastic
	rs				pnt_tray_spec
	alphax			0.25
	alphay			0.25
	ior				2.5
	extinction		3.0
}

lambertian_material
{
	name			mat_floor
	reflectance		pnt_floor
}

##################################################
# Objects -- bench
##################################################

##################################################
# THE TOP IS EIGHT BOARDS, NOT A BOX.
#
# The single `box_geometry` this replaced could not be a bench top at this
# camera distance for a reason that has nothing to do with painters: the
# structure a 10.7 mm pixel CAN resolve on a bench top is its boards, and a
# box has none.  Eight of them, on a slab that ties them together:
#
#   part 1      the slab       3.000 x 0.115 x 1.100 m, local y -0.060..0.055
#   parts 2-9   the boards     3.000 x 0.022 x 0.13313 m, local y 0.038..0.060,
#                              arrises eased 9 mm, centres 138.125 mm apart
#                              from z = -0.4834375 -- so the outer flanks of
#                              the first and last land exactly on z = +-0.55
#                              and the overall bbox is the 3.0 x 0.12 x 1.1
#                              the box had.  Nothing else in the scene moved.
#
# The joint is therefore a 5 mm gap between two 9 mm eased arrises, on boards
# standing 5 mm proud of the slab: a 23 mm valley (2.1 px) that is 5 mm deep,
# opening onto the slab rather than running through the top.  ALL THREE OF
# THOSE NUMBERS WERE ARRIVED AT BY RENDERING.  The first cut was an 8 mm gap
# 10 mm deep between free-standing boards, and it rendered the joints
# NEAR-BLACK -- no sky reaches an 8 x 120 mm slot under direct lighting, and
# eight black lines across a bench top is the duckboard it looked like.  The
# opposite cut, 3 mm and 2 mm, was INVISIBLE: at 10.7 mm per pixel a 3 mm
# groove is antialiased away entirely, and the joints that seemed to appear
# when it was tried turned out to be a relief artefact rather than the
# groove.  What reads is a shallow eased VALLEY -- wide enough for the sky to
# reach its floor, deep enough to shade.  The dark line you see is mostly the
# dirt in `expr_top`; the geometry's job is to make that dirt land in a real
# crease and to give `convexity()` an arris to find.
#
# WHAT THE SDF BUYS, beyond the boards themselves: `convexity()` answers on
# it.  On a `box_geometry` it returns its neutral 0 and `occlusion()` its
# neutral 1, so doctrine 3's edge-wear term is simply unavailable -- only
# the SDF family and indexed meshes answer those.  The arris polish along
# every joint lip and the whole front edge is that signal.
#
# Cost: this is the scene's slowest object to intersect (a ray-marched
# 3 m slab that fills the frame).  Rasterization went 2.44 s -> 4.35 s at
# 640x480x12, which is the whole of this rework's cost.
##################################################

sdf_geometry
{
	name			geo_bench_top
	part			box       union  0   0 -0.0025 0          0 0 0   1 1 1   1.5  0.0575 0.55     0
	part			roundbox  union  0   0  0.049 -0.483438  0 0 0   1 1 1   1.5  0.011  0.0665625  0.009
	part			roundbox  union  0   0  0.049 -0.345313  0 0 0   1 1 1   1.5  0.011  0.0665625  0.009
	part			roundbox  union  0   0  0.049 -0.207187  0 0 0   1 1 1   1.5  0.011  0.0665625  0.009
	part			roundbox  union  0   0  0.049 -0.0690625 0 0 0   1 1 1   1.5  0.011  0.0665625  0.009
	part			roundbox  union  0   0  0.049 0.0690625  0 0 0   1 1 1   1.5  0.011  0.0665625  0.009
	part			roundbox  union  0   0  0.049 0.207187   0 0 0   1 1 1   1.5  0.011  0.0665625  0.009
	part			roundbox  union  0   0  0.049 0.345313   0 0 0   1 1 1   1.5  0.011  0.0665625  0.009
	part			roundbox  union  0   0  0.049 0.483438   0 0 0   1 1 1   1.5  0.011  0.0665625  0.009
	maxsteps		160
}

standard_object
{
	name			obj_bench_top
	geometry		geo_bench_top
	material		mat_wood_top
	position		0 0.85 0
	modifier		relief_wood
}

box_geometry
{
	name			geo_leg
	width			0.12
	height			0.8
	depth			0.12
}

standard_object
{
	name			obj_leg_fl
	geometry		geo_leg
	material		mat_wood_leg
	position		-1.35 0.4 -0.45
	modifier		relief_leg
}

standard_object
{
	name			obj_leg_fr
	geometry		geo_leg
	material		mat_wood_leg
	position		1.35 0.4 -0.45
	modifier		relief_leg
}

standard_object
{
	name			obj_leg_bl
	geometry		geo_leg
	material		mat_wood_leg
	position		-1.35 0.4 0.45
	modifier		relief_leg
}

standard_object
{
	name			obj_leg_br
	geometry		geo_leg
	material		mat_wood_leg
	position		1.35 0.4 0.45
	modifier		relief_leg
}

##################################################
# Objects -- the bench vise (bolted to the right end of the bench).
#
# DOCTRINE 7 (plank_closeup.RISEscene's header): the silhouette has to say
# what the object is before a single painter is bound to it.  Local frame:
# +Y up, +Z out along the screw axis, origin on the flange's underside, so
# the object below simply drops local y = 0 onto the bench top at y = 0.91.
#
#  1  base flange              roundbox, 390 x 42 x 344 mm, 18 mm rounding -- half-extents
#                              (0.195, 0.021, 0.172) m, and RISE's `roundbox` INSETS the
#                           rounding, so those ARE the outer half-extents.  The header                      #
#                           said "28 x ... 12 mm arris" until 2026-09-08; both numbers                      #
#                           were wrong against this line.  The 18 mm quarter-round on a                     #
#                           21 mm half-height is what puts the flange's touchdown line                      #
#                           18 mm INSIDE the nominal edge, which is where the grime                         #
#                           profile in `expr_top` is measured from.                                         #
#  2  cast body                roundbox, smin k 30 mm to the flange -- the
#                              fillet a casting is drawn with, and the flat
#                           top behind the jaw is the anvil pad                                             #
#  3  fixed rear jaw           roundbox, smin k 24 mm, rising out of 2
#  4  slide bar                box, HARD union: a machined member meets a
#                              casting at an edge, never at a fillet
#  5  movable jaw body         roundbox, smin k 21 mm, wrapping 4
#  6  movable front jaw        roundbox, smin k 24 mm
#  7  rear jaw pad             box, hard union, standing 17 mm PROUD of the
#  8  front jaw pad            casting -- see the mask note below
#  9  lead screw               cylinder on Z, emerging from 5
# 10  thrust collar            cylinder
# 11  T-handle                 capsule, hung -35 deg and slid 45 mm off
#                              centre in its hole
# 12-15  bolt heads            cylinders standing 18 mm proud of the flange
#
# The jaws stand 56 mm apart (10 px) and the pads 24 mm (4 px): the gap is
# the second thing after the handle that says "vise", so it is sized to be
# seen, not to be right.
#
# `orientation 0 48 0` -- a vise has a swivel base precisely so it can sit
# at an angle to the bench, and 48 degrees is the yaw that puts the jaw
# gap, the jaw faces and the whole length of the handle in shot at once.
##################################################

sdf_geometry
{
	name			geo_vise
	part			roundbox  union     0        0       0.021  -0.030     0 0 0     1 1 1    0.195  0.021  0.172    0.018
	part			roundbox  smin      0.030    0       0.114  -0.078     0 0 0     1 1 1    0.120  0.093  0.093    0.027
	part			roundbox  smin      0.024    0       0.219  -0.054     0 0 0     1 1 1    0.135  0.081  0.039    0.015
	part			box       union     0        0       0.150   0.030     0 0 0     1 1 1    0.054  0.039  0.128    0
